  "ground_truth": {
    "root_cause": "notification-worker has a memory leak: an in-memory de-duplication set that is never bounded or evicted (observed holding 14M entries). As notification volume grew, the set's footprint climbed until the container hit its memory limit and was OOM-killed, restarting on a sawtooth. During each growth phase, GC pressure slowed processing and the work queue backed up. The leak predates today - the last deploy was 3 days ago - and only became visible now as volume crossed the threshold. Recovery required bounding the set with a size cap and TTL.",
    "evidence_path": [
      "notification-worker heap climbs steadily from ~45%, independent of queue depth - a leak signature, not load",
      "repeated OOMKilled restarts on a sawtooth (restart #1 ... #4)",
      "the dedup set is observed holding 14M entries and never evicting",
      "queue_lag grows during each heap-growth phase from GC pressure",
      "no deploy today; the last deploy was 3 days ago",
      "heap stabilises after a size bound and TTL are added to the set"
    ],
    "fix": "Bound the dedup set with a max size and TTL (done). Add a heap-growth-rate alert, audit other unbounded in-memory collections, and set the container memory limit from the real bounded working set.",
    "should_escalate": false
  }
